  "notes": "The stanza of the Hebrew letter HE — psalm 118 is an alphabetic acrostic of twenty-two stanzas of eight verses, one per letter, and every verse of this one begins with H in the Hebrew. Verse 34 is the verse this edition is named from: DA MIHI INTELLECTUM, ET SCRUTABOR LEGEM TUAM. Three constructions are worth naming. LEGEM (w001) is not the object of PONE: the object is VIAM (w005), and legem is a predicative accusative saying what the way is to become — the Greek has one verb here, νομοθετέω, to lay down as law, which the Latin spreads over two words. EXQUIRAM (w009) and CUSTODIAM (w020) are each future indicative and present subjunctive at once — I will seek / let me seek — and nothing in the sentence decides between them, so this edition records person, number and voice and declines to state tense or mood, as it does for retribuam in the communion psalm. SUSPICATUS SUM (w068-w069) is a deponent perfect: passive in form, active in meaning, so it says I have feared and not I have been suspected. The psalm is said in the Roman breviary at Terce on Sunday; it carries no speaker, because a psalm said in choir has none this edition would record.",
  "source": {
    "method": "Verified by collation against the two independent witnesses below: zero substantive divergence over all eight verses. The only differences are the consonantal i, which witness do prints as j (iustificatiónum, iudícia, iucúnda), and punctuation.",
